@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Minimal internal-BDD showing two blocks whose internal
'                 parts exchange a typed item flow through flow ports.
'   usecase:      Teach flow ports + item flows across block boundaries.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       sysml internal block definition / fluid transfer
' ============================================================================
title Example Internal BDD - Simple Internal Flow

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Mapping note — how the SysML concepts map onto this PlantUML sketch.
' PlantUML has no native SysML, so we approximate:
'   - block               -> class <<block>>
'   - part (usage)        -> object rectangle inside the owning block
'   - flow port           -> boundary stud drawn with () on the block edge
'   - connector           -> --> edge between two ports
'   - item flow           -> typed label on the connector edge plus a note
'                            naming the flow properties carried
'   - flow direction      -> direction arrow on the edge (-> out, <- in)
' In a real IBD this is the interior of a single block; an INTERNAL-BDD
' instead shows the internal parts/ports of SEVERAL block definitions and
' the item flows that cross from one block's interior to another's.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
note over "TankBlock", "PumpBlock"
  Flow port (drawn as "()") carries a typed
  item flow. Direction is shown by the edge
  arrow; the label names the flow type and
  the flow properties it conveys.
end note

class "TankBlock" <<block>> {
  +out: FluidPort
  part supply : FluidStore
}

class "PumpBlock" <<block>> {
  +in: FluidPort
  part intake : IntakeChamber
}

' Flow ports are shown as studs on each block boundary.
' Connector between the two flow ports carries the typed item flow.
' "FluidFlow{rate, pressure}" lists the flow properties carried.
"TankBlock" -(#darkblue)->[color=#darkblue] "PumpBlock" : FluidFlow { rate, pressure }

note right of "TankBlock"
  TankBlock.out is a FLOW PORT (atomic,
  type = FluidFlow). It exports the
  flow properties rate, pressure.
end note

note right of "PumpBlock"
  PumpBlock.in is a FLOW PORT that
  receives FluidFlow. Direction of the
  edge encodes out->in flow.
end note

@enduml
